# Ash-first semantic mapping

This guide starts with ordinary Ash resources and adds only the semantic information required to expose them as a virtual RDF graph.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install(
  [
    {:ash, "~> 3.0"},
    {:ash_postgres, "~> 2.0"},
    {:ash_r2ml, "~> 1.0"}
  ],
  consolidate_protocols: false
)
```

AshR2ML is an extension, not a data layer.

```elixir
defmodule Example.Person do
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: Example.Domain,
    data_layer: AshPostgres.DataLayer,
    extensions: [AshR2ML.Resource]
end
```

## Define the domain

```elixir
defmodule Example.Domain do
  use Ash.Domain

  resources do
    resource Example.Person
    resource Example.Organization
  end
end
```

## Define a mapped organization

```elixir
defmodule Example.Organization do
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: Example.Domain,
    data_layer: AshPostgres.DataLayer,
    extensions: [AshR2ML.Resource]

  postgres do
    table "organizations"
    repo Example.Repo
  end

  r2rml do
    class "https://schema.org/Organization"

    subject do
      template "https://example.org/organizations/{id}"
      term_type :iri
    end
  end

  attributes do
    uuid_primary_key :id

    attribute :name, :string do
      allow_nil? false
      public? true

      rdf do
        predicate "https://schema.org/name"
      end
    end
  end
end
```

The important division is:

```text
Ash/AshPostgres knows:
  table
  column
  type
  nullability
  identity

AshR2ML adds:
  RDF class
  predicate
  subject IRI construction
```

## Define a relationship

```elixir
defmodule Example.Person do
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: Example.Domain,
    data_layer: AshPostgres.DataLayer,
    extensions: [AshR2ML.Resource]

  postgres do
    table "people"
    repo Example.Repo
  end

  r2rml do
    class "http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/Person"

    subject do
      template "https://example.org/people/{id}"
      term_type :iri
    end
  end

  attributes do
    uuid_primary_key :id

    attribute :name, :string do
      allow_nil? false
      public? true

      rdf do
        predicate "http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/name"
      end
    end
  end

  relationships do
    belongs_to :organization, Example.Organization do
      allow_nil? false
      public? true

      rdf do
        predicate "https://schema.org/memberOf"
      end
    end
  end
end
```

AshPostgres owns the relational relationship:

```text
people.organization_id → organizations.id
```

AshR2ML compiles the semantic relationship:

```text
<PersonIRI> schema:memberOf <OrganizationIRI>
```

No second relationship declaration is needed.

## Inspect the normalized mapping

```elixir
person_mapping = AshR2ML.Resource.Info.mapping(Example.Person)
organization_mapping = AshR2ML.Resource.Info.mapping(Example.Organization)

{person_mapping, organization_mapping}
```

The person mapping contains:

```text
logical table: people
class: foaf:Person
subject template: https://example.org/people/{id}
scalar predicate: foaf:name ← people.name
relationship: schema:memberOf
join: people.organization_id → organizations.id
parent triples map: Organization
```

## Generate R2RML

```elixir
{:ok, turtle} =
  AshR2ML.R2RML.render([
    Example.Organization,
    Example.Person
  ])

IO.puts(turtle)
```

The relationship is emitted as a reference object map rather than as a duplicated scalar foreign-key predicate.

## Semantic identity is independent from database identity

A resource can use a composite business identity in its subject template.

```elixir
r2rml do
  class "https://example.org/Account"

  subject do
    template "https://example.org/accounts/{tenant_id}/{account_number}"
    term_type :iri
  end
end
```

AshR2ML verifies both fields exist. If the configured subject construction is not deterministic for the resource, compilation fails.

## Override an RDF datatype

AshR2ML normally derives RDF datatypes from Ash types.

Use an override only when the semantic datatype differs from the default registry and the conversion is lawful:

```elixir
attribute :amount, :decimal do
  rdf do
    predicate "https://example.org/amount"
    datatype "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal"
  end
end
```

An incompatible override is refused.

## Many-to-many

A standard Ash many-to-many relationship can be mapped when AshR2ML can introspect the join resource/relationship and both semantic identities.

```text
Post --taggedWith--> Tag
```

The relational path may be:

```text
posts
  ↑
post_tags
  ↓
tags
```

AshR2ML emits a semantic object-property mapping that follows the join rather than exposing the join table as accidental RDF structure.

When the join carries real domain facts, model it as a first-class mapped resource instead.

## Validate before publishing mappings

```elixir
:ok = AshR2ML.verify([Example.Organization, Example.Person])
{:ok, turtle} = AshR2ML.R2RML.render([Example.Organization, Example.Person])
```

Verification checks include:

- valid class and predicate IRIs,
- exactly one effective subject construction,
- template fields resolve,
- datatypes are supported,
- relationship destinations are mapped,
- relational join keys are resolvable,
- destination semantic identity is available,
- no mapping is silently omitted.

## Use with an OBDA engine

Write the mapping to a deployment artifact:

```elixir
File.mkdir_p!("priv/r2rml")
File.write!("priv/r2rml/application.ttl", turtle)
```

Point a compatible R2RML/OBDA engine at:

```text
PostgreSQL connection
+
priv/r2rml/application.ttl
```

Then a SPARQL query such as:

```sparql
SELECT ?person ?organization
WHERE {
  ?person <https://schema.org/memberOf> ?organization .
}
```

is rewritten to SQL over the same relational state Ash uses.

## Verification crown

The semantic mapping is complete when the same fixture is observable through both paths:

```text
Ash.get!/Ash.read!
      │
      ├── Person id/name/organization
      │
PostgreSQL
      │
      └── R2RML → SPARQL
            Person IRI/name/organization IRI
```

The identifiers and required relationships must correspond exactly under the configured subject maps.
